WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.366
Validation starts before code runs.

2
00:00:02.366 --> 00:00:17.174
Teams need a clear definition of expected data: which fields are required, which fields are optional, how long strings may be, which numeric ranges are valid, which formats are accepted, and which values belong to a controlled set.

3
00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:21.906
C# types express some of that contract, but they do not express every rule.

4
00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:31.447
A string property says little about length, allowed characters, business meaning, or whether the value should be present for a specific operation.

5
00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:37.934
Nullable reference types help make intent visible, but they are not a complete validation strategy.

6
00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:45.567
They can show that a value should not be null in well-typed code, and they can reduce accidental null handling mistakes.

7
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:57.092
External input still needs runtime checks because deserialization, model binding, configuration, and reflection-based frameworks can produce missing or unexpected values.

8
00:00:57.092 --> 00:01:01.367
Nullable annotations should support validation, not replace it.

9
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:04.573
Common data categories each need their own rules.

10
00:01:04.573 --> 00:01:10.984
GUIDs should be parsed and checked for allowed use, not just accepted because they have a GUID shape.

11
00:01:10.984 --> 00:01:15.258
Dates and times need format, range, time zone, and ordering rules.

12
00:01:15.258 --> 00:01:23.960
Enums should be constrained to intended values, especially when numeric binding can produce values outside normal business options.

13
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:30.524
Collections need limits, item validation, duplicate rules, and sometimes cross-item constraints.

14
00:01:30.524 --> 00:01:34.264
The strongest contracts combine structure with meaning.

15
00:01:34.264 --> 00:01:41.515
A request may contain a valid start date and a valid end date, but the end date still needs to be after the start date.

16
00:01:41.515 --> 00:01:46.323
A quantity may be an integer, but it may exceed an inventory or policy limit.

17
00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:50.369
A field may be optional in one workflow and required in another.

18
00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:58.154
Defining these expectations before processing data makes validators easier to test and business logic easier to trust.
